Same regime as (3/3), split out only because the content operand had to be
reconstructed rather than read straight off the call.

  _hashseed(X) and _hs(X) are both
      int.from_bytes(_dig(",".join(str(x) for x in X))[:8], "big")
  so the thing actually being addressed is that join over X. Six sites in
  882/883/889 address a numeric coordinate — a quaternion product, an octonion
  context window, a page position. Coordinates owe each other no edit-distance,
  so addressing is the right regime and the hand-rolled digest was simply
  klein4_address written out longhand.

  863 keyed a token as seed=sum(t.encode()) — an ORDER-INSENSITIVE byte sum.
  Every anagram collides outright ("dog"/"god" got the same vector), and so does
  any permutation. That is not a weak address, it is a broken one, and it is now
  klein4_address(D, t).

Numbers change at all 7. 4 files, all byte-compile.

